                  "text": "### 🏛️ Engineering Diagnosis: Parametric BIM vs Generative 3D\n\nWhen Gemini chat produces an impressive 3D scene, it typically writes arbitrary Three.js scripts, loads unconstrained polygonal meshes (GLTF/OBJ), or applies custom GLSL shaders and studio lighting. \n\nX-Ray operates under an entirely different paradigm: it is an authoritative, multi-layer parametric BIM kernel (`xray.architect/v1`). Every element must maintain strict topological relationships, layer assemblies, level datums, opening host constraints, and DXF/IFC export integrity.\n\n```mindmap\nX-Ray Architectural Capabilities\n  Hard Schema & Engine Limits\n    2.5D Primitives Only\n    No Non-Wall Openings or Free Meshes\n    Strict Batch Size and Convex Roofs\n  Achievable but Laborious\n    Faceted Organic Geometry\n    Multi-Tier Cantilevers & Lightwells\n    Rhythmic Facades & Complex Fenestration\n  Workflow & Visual Gaps\n    Standardized CAD Shading vs Ray Tracing\n    Assistant Prompt Conservatism\n  Unverifiable Claims\n    Client WebGL Performance\n    Subjective Aesthetic Impressions\n```\n\n---\n\n### 1. Hard Schema and Engine Limitations\n\nThese are mechanical boundaries enforced by the workbench schema and tool APIs (`read_workbench_structure`, `draw_architect_elements`):\n\n* **Limited Authored Primitives**: `draw_architect_elements` only accepts `level`, `wall`, `door`, `window`, `slab`, `roof`, `line`, `room`, `footprint`, and `extrude`. It cannot author columns, stairs, railings, curtain walls, solar panels, or site terrain—even though some of these exist as read-only categories in cataloged source reconstructions.\n* **Strict Planar and Host Constraints**:\n  * **Walls** are strictly vertical linear segments ($A \\to B$) between level boundaries. Slanted walls, battered walls, or spline surfaces cannot be expressed.\n  * **Openings** (`door`, `window`) can only exist if hosted inside a wall segment with a linear offset, width, height, and sill. Free-st