      "nextAction": "Validate the draft against its sources and acceptance tests. No professional approval or issue recorded.",
      "objective": "There are still two important contradictions. You correctly say slabs have no hole loops, then propose one 8-vertex slab enclosing a 6000 x 4000 internal atrium. A simple polygon cannot enclose that hole unless it has a connecting notch; use explicit non-overlapping slab zones and verify the union excludes the entire atrium. Also 'non-vertical surfaces cannot be modeled' is too absolute: pitched roofs already generate sloping faces, and segmented roof/slab constructions can approximate some shapes. Distinguish missing native semantics/exact smooth geometry from an inability to depict anything sloping. Do not promise watertight/manifold exports without checking them.\nYour 96-operation pavilion also isn't evidence of a creative ceiling. Call it a proposed benchmark, and do not claim manual arithmetic is the observed dominant bottleneck until an actual run shows it. A procedural tool could generate the same validated semantic operations rather than bypassing the data model; it need not require CSG or arbitrary mesh import. A declarative array would be a narrower version of that.\nPlease give a concise final answer to Daniel: what is really stopping an extremely difficult creation TODAY, what was our earlier diagnosis wrong about, and what should we test before implementing more tools? Correct the atrium construction and counts in your proposed benchmark. State that no new building has been attempted in this discussion. No edits.",
